    "statutory": {
      "level": "No Known Issue",
      "evidence": "**Evidence:**\n- The RMP collection is **explicitly and specifically required by statute**: Clean Air Act **\u00a7112(r)(7)** (42 U.S.C. 7412(r)(7)) directs EPA to promulgate accident-prevention regulations and requires owners/operators to prepare and submit Risk Management Plans to EPA.\n- **Public availability is also statutory**: CAA \u00a7112(r)(7)(B)(iii) makes RMPs available to the public, subject to the narrow OCA security restrictions added by the **Chemical Safety Information, Site Security and Fuels Regulatory Relief Act (CSISSFRRA), P.L. 106-40** (1999).\n- Implementing regulations are codified at **40 CFR Part 68** (collection requirements) and **40 CFR Part 1400** (OCA access restrictions).\n- There is **no alternative federal data collection that substitutes** for RMP accident-prevention, chemical-inventory, and five-year accident-history data. (TRI and OSHA PSM overlap conceptually but do not duplicate RMP's accident-history and hazard-assessment content.)\n- Programmatic use is clear: LEPC/SERC emergency planning, EPA/state compliance, and community right-to-know.\n\n**Selected risk level: No Known Issue.** The data are statutorily required, the statutory authority is explicitly named and specific about what must be collected, and no substitute collection exists.\n\n---"
    },
