    "future": {
      "level": "High Risk",
      "evidence": "**Evidence:**\n- The FY2026 President's Budget (Congressional Budget Justification) proposes a **$40 million cut** to the Current Surveys line that funds SIPP and states SIPP is *\"transitioning to a more effective approach that merges modern survey techniques with advanced data science methods\"* (The Census Project, June 30, 2025; CRS IF12714).\n- A Federal Register notice (Aug 7, 2026; 91 FR 51147, Doc. 2026-16188) formally announces that the Census Bureau will seek OMB clearance for a **calendar-year 2027 SIPP field test of only ~6,000 respondents \"in lieu of a national collection using the questionnaires, systems, and processes in place since 2014.\"** That is a documented plan to skip the national collection in 2027.\n- The current ICR (0607-1000) **expires 12/31/2026**; the replacement request is a \"New Information Collection Request\" for a drastically smaller collection.\n- **Countervailing facts:** The 2025 SIPP was released on schedule (July 15, 2026), and a 2026 collection cycle appears to have occurred. Collection has **not** been terminated.\n\n**Finding:** The most recent release was on time, but a national collection skip is formally noticed for 2027 and the ICR is set to lapse at year-end 2026 with only a small field test replacing it. This meets the rubric's \"collection or publication skipped\" criterion (documented/upcoming). *High Risk.*"
    },
    "quality": {
      "level": "High Risk",
      "evidence": "**Evidence:**\n- The Census Bureau's own 2025 SIPP materials state the survey *\"continued to face data collection complications because of increasing costs and a general lower-than-average unit response rate nationally\"* (2025 SIPP data page; 2025 Users' Guide).\n- Effective sample size has already collapsed: roughly **14,000 interviewed households** in the 2025 collection versus a ~53,000 household design target used for the 2022 panel \u2014 a de facto ~70%+ reduction. Weighted wave response rates are in the **~37\u201349% range**.\n- The planned 2027 redesign cuts granularity further: **6-month reference period** (vs. 12-month), **one adult answering for the whole household**, **web-first/self-response mode**, and a **6,000-case field test** in place of the national survey.\n- Historical note: the 2019 panel was discontinued after one wave.\n\n**Finding:** Reductions in sample size, response quality, and (planned) reference-period and mode granularity are already occurring and formally proposed. *High Risk.*"
    },
    "statutory": {
      "level": "Moderate Risk",
      "evidence": "**Evidence:**\n- SIPP is authorized under **Title 13, U.S.C. \u00a7 182**, which permits (but does not mandate) the Secretary of Commerce to collect income and program participation data. It is **not a statutorily required** survey.\n- SIPP is, however, widely relied upon for **federal program implementation and oversight**: SSA benefit/retirement-income projections, USDA/SNAP eligibility analysis, CBO scoring of program costs, and, per CBPP, information used for federally required reports to Congress.\n- **No full substitute exists** for SIPP's monthly, longitudinal, program-participation detail; the administration's proposed reliance on administrative records is widely assessed (CBPP, CNSTAT) as unable to replicate that content.\n\n**Finding:** Authorization is explicitly named but permissive, and the survey supports federal program implementation without a complete alternative \u2014 fitting the \"Moderate Risk\" definition rather than \"No Known Issue\" (not statutorily required) or \"High Risk\" (authorization is not vague and programmatic use is clear). *Moderate Risk.*"
    },
    "staffing": {
      "level": "Moderate Risk",
      "evidence": "**Evidence:**\n- The American Statistical Association reports the **Census Bureau has lost an estimated ~15% of staffing since FY2024**, and the FY26 request is for staffing 9% below FY24 (AMSTAT, \"The Nation's Data at Risk\").\n- Funding: FY25 was flat (est. ~2.4% purchasing-power loss); the FY26 request cuts the **Current Surveys account by ~12.3%** ($329M \u2192 $289M), and the **Current Demographic Statistics line (which funds SIPP) by ~35%** ($113M \u2192 $73M) \u2014 though the enacted outcome is not confirmed in the sources retrieved.\n- Leadership instability: a **political appointee (George Cook) was named acting Census director** in September 2025; at least five division/office chiefs departed; four Census advisory committees were terminated (NPR, AMSTAT). ~100 Census call-center employees received RIF notices in the October 2025 shutdown; Commerce issued ~315 RIF notices (The Census Project, Oct 14, 2025).\n- The USAJobs federal staffing tracker (usajobs-tracker.abigailhaddad.com) confirms a government-wide hiring slowdown and was itself archived Dec 31, 2025; it does not yield reliable SIPP-specific counts.\n\n**Finding:** Census-wide staff loss (~15%) and the current-surveys account cut (~12%) fall in the 10\u201339%/10\u201319% bands, with threatened/realized leadership turnover. The SIPP-specific proposed cut (~35%) is a significant escalator toward High if enacted. *Moderate Risk.*"
    },
    "policy": {
      "level": "High Risk",
      "evidence": "**Evidence:**\n- The President's FY2026 budget **explicitly proposes major structural changes to SIPP** \u2014 a smaller targeted sample plus non-survey/administrative data \u2014 squarely in line with administration priorities (Census CBJ via The Census Project; CRS IF12714).\n- The Census Bureau has advanced the change through a formal **new information-collection request** for the 2027 field test replacing national collection (Federal Register, Aug 7, 2026).\n- Broader administration activity: **DOGE's \"survey of surveys\"** review of 100+ Census surveys and termination of five (May 2025, AP News), plus a presidential directive to alter the decennial census's counting basis (AMSTAT) \u2014 indicating active, high-level pressure on Census data collection.\n- Advocacy groups (CBPP, AMSTAT, The Census Project) are actively warning that SIPP is targeted for cutback.\n\n**Finding:** Significant, administration-driven changes to what SIPP collects and how it is fielded are already reflected in budget documents and a formal ICR. *High Risk.*\n\n---"
    }
